= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Bring all terms to the left and factor the <quadratic polynomial>:
$$
2x^2+x-6=(2x-3)(x+2).
$$
Its leading coefficient is positive, so it is nonpositive between its two <polynomial roots>, including the endpoints. Therefore
$$
\boxed{-2\le x\le\frac32.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Complete the squares in the two coordinates:
$$
(x-2)^2-4+4(y-1)^2-4=-4,
\qquad\frac{(x-2)^2}{4}+(y-1)^2=1.
$$
This standard equation of an <ellipse> is symmetric about $x=2$ and $y=1$. Its centre is therefore
$$
\boxed{(2,1).}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At a point of tangency, the <derivative> of the curve equals the <slope> one of the straight line. The <chain rule> gives $2\cos2x=1$. Since $0\le2x\le\pi$, the unique solution is $x=\pi/6$. The corresponding height is $\sin(\pi/3)=\sqrt3/2$. Substitution in the <tangent line> gives
$$
\boxed{c=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}-\frac\pi6.}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <normal vector> direction is <orthogonal> to the <tangent line>. For finite nonzero <slopes>, their product is $-1$. The tangent has <slope> one, so the normal line has
$$
\boxed{\text{slope}=-1.}
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The two equal legs meet at the <right angle>, so the <Pythagorean theorem> gives $AB^2+AC^2=BC^2=4$, with $AB=AC$. Thus $AC=\sqrt2$. The midpoint condition then yields
$$
\boxed{AD=\frac{AC}{2}=\frac1{\sqrt2}\text{ units}.}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The triangle $ADB$ is right-angled at $A$, with $AB=\sqrt2$ and $AD=1/\sqrt2$. Another application of the <Pythagorean theorem> gives $DB=\sqrt{2+1/2}=\sqrt{5/2}$. At the <angle> with vertex $D$, the adjacent side is $AD$ and the hypotenuse is $DB$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\cos\angle ADB=\frac{AD}{DB}=\frac1{\sqrt5}.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The <double-angle formula> gives $\cos2x=1-2\sin^2x$. Setting $s=\sin x$ reduces the equation to $2s^2+s-1=(2s-1)(s+1)=0$. On $0\le x\le\pi$, the <sine> is nonnegative, so only $s=1/2$ is allowed. Therefore
$$
\boxed{x=\frac\pi6\quad\text{or}\quad x=\frac{5\pi}6.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Use <integration by parts> with $u=\ln x$ and $dv=x^2\,dx$. This gives the <antiderivative>
$$
\int x^2\ln x\,dx=\frac{x^3}{3}\ln x-\frac13\int x^2\,dx
=\frac{x^3}{3}\ln x-\frac{x^3}{9}+C.
$$
Evaluating at the two bounds, with $\ln1=0$, yields
$$
\boxed{\int_1^y x^2\ln x\,dx=\frac{y^3}{3}\ln y-\frac{y^3-1}{9}.}
$$

