= Solution
{parent=a}

There are $100$ terms in this <arithmetic series>, starting at $4$ and ending at $202$. Pairing the first and last terms, the second and penultimate terms, and so on, gives
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=2}^{101}2n=\frac{100}{2}(4+202)=10300.}
$$
The summand in the PDF is $2n$, not $2^n$.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

This is a <finite geometric series> with first term and common ratio both equal to four. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^{5}4^n=\frac{4(4^5-1)}{4-1}=1364.}
$$

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Multiply the <partial fraction decomposition> by $(x+1)(x+2)^2$ to obtain a <polynomial identity>:
$$
x^2+1=a(x+2)^2+b(x+1)(x+2)-5(x+1).
$$
At $x=-1$, this gives $a=2$. Comparing the coefficients of $x^2$ then gives $a+b=1$, so
$$
\boxed{a=2,\qquad b=-1.}
$$
The remaining coefficients agree: $4a+3b-5=0$ and $4a+2b-5=1$. The original rational identity is understood away from its <poles> $-1$ and $-2$.

