= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $M=2x+e^y$ and $N=xe^y-\cos y$. Their cross <partial derivatives> agree: $M_y=e^y=N_x$. On the <simply connected> plane, this makes the <differential form> exact. Integrating $F_x=M$ gives $F=x^2+xe^y+h(y)$; matching $F_y=N$ then requires $h'(y)=-\cos y$, so one potential is
$$
F(x,y)=x^2+xe^y-\sin y.
$$
The <ordinary differential equation> states $dF/dx=0$ along a solution. Its <initial condition> fixes the constant to $F(1,\pi/2)=e^{\pi/2}$. Hence the solution is specified implicitly by
$$
\boxed{x^2+xe^y-\sin y=e^{\pi/2},\qquad y(1)=\frac\pi2.}
$$
At the initial point $F_y=e^{\pi/2}\ne0$, so the <implicit function theorem> guarantees a unique local branch $y(x)$ through that point.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $x\ne0$, multiply by $x$ to obtain the first-order <linear ordinary differential equation> $y'+xy=5xe^{-x^2}$. Its <integrating factor> is $\mu=e^{x^2/2}$. The <product rule> then gives
$$
(ye^{x^2/2})'=5xe^{-x^2/2}.
$$
Integration yields $ye^{x^2/2}=-5e^{-x^2/2}+C$, so
$$
\boxed{y(x)=Ce^{-x^2/2}-5e^{-x^2},\qquad x\ne0.}
$$
The <constant of integration> is arbitrary on each connected interval of the original equation's domain. The formula extends smoothly through zero, but the printed equation itself contains $1/x$ and is not defined there.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The real equation has domain $x>0$. Its <integrating factor> is
$$
\mu(x)=\exp\left(\int(1+\ln x)\,dx\right)=e^{x\ln x}=x^x.
$$
Thus the <linear ordinary differential equation> becomes $(x^xy)'=x^xx^{-x}=1$. After integration, $x^xy=x+C$. The <initial condition> gives $2=1+C$, hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=(x+1)x^{-x},\qquad x>0.}
$$

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write the <scalar field> as $u=R(r)Y(\theta,\phi)$, where $R=r+r^{-2}$ and $Y=\sin\theta\cos\phi$. Its radial contribution to the <Laplacian> in <spherical coordinates> is
$$
\frac{Y}{r^2}(r^2R')'=Y\left(\frac2r+\frac2{r^4}\right)=\frac{2R}{r^2}Y.
$$
For the angular contribution, $Y_\theta=\cos\theta\cos\phi$ and $Y_{\phi\phi}=-\sin\theta\cos\phi$, so
$$
\frac1{\sin\theta}\partial_\theta(\sin\theta\,Y_\theta)
+\frac{Y_{\phi\phi}}{\sin^2\theta}
=\frac{\cos\phi}{\sin\theta}(\cos^2\theta-\sin^2\theta-1)=-2Y.
$$
Combining the radial and angular terms proves
$$
\boxed{\nabla^2u=\frac{2R}{r^2}Y-\frac{2R}{r^2}Y=0\qquad(r>0).}
$$
The calculation uses $0<\theta<\pi$ to avoid coordinate singularities. In <Cartesian coordinates>, $u=x(1+r^{-3})$, which extends smoothly across the polar axis for $r>0$. It therefore satisfies <Laplace's equation> there as well. The origin is a genuine singularity and is excluded.

