= Solution
{parent=a}

The finite form of <Taylor's theorem> is
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k+R_n(x),\qquad R_n(x)=\frac{f^{(n)}(\xi)}{n!}(x-a)^n,}
$$
where $\xi$ lies between $a$ and $x$ when $x\ne a$. Sufficient hypotheses are continuous <derivatives> up to order $n-1$ on the interval joining the endpoints and an $n$th <derivative> in its interior. The first $n$ terms end at degree $n-1$; the <Taylor remainder> uses the $n$th <derivative>. At $x=a$ the remainder is zero. This finite <Taylor polynomial> identity does not require convergence of an infinite <Taylor series>.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write the <sine> argument as $\pi/2+\delta$, with $\delta=\tfrac\pi2(e^x-1)=\tfrac\pi2x+\tfrac\pi4x^2+O(x^3)$. The <sine addition formula> gives $\sin(\pi/2+\delta)=\cos\delta$. Its <Maclaurin series> is $1-\delta^2/2+O(\delta^4)$, so
$$
\boxed{\sin\left(\frac{\pi e^x}{2}\right)=1-\frac{\pi^2}{8}x^2+O(x^3).}
$$
There is no linear term because the outer <sine> has zero <derivative> at $\pi/2$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $h=x+1$, the displacement from the expansion point. The numerator has <Taylor series> $\sinh h=h+h^3/6+O(h^5)$, while the reciprocal denominator has <geometric series> $(1+h)^{-1}=1-h+h^2-h^3+h^4+O(h^5)$. Multiplication gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sinh(x+1)}{x+2}=(x+1)-(x+1)^2+\frac76(x+1)^3-\frac76(x+1)^4+O((x+1)^5).}
$$
The local <power series> converges for $|x+1|<1$, the distance to the denominator's zero at $x=-2$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the <Maclaurin series> of the <logarithm> and <hyperbolic cosine>:
$$
\ln(1+x^3)=x^3-\frac12x^6+O(x^9),\qquad
\frac1{\cosh x}=1-\frac12x^2+\frac5{24}x^4+O(x^6).
$$
To retain terms through degree six, only the constant and quadratic terms of the reciprocal factor can multiply the leading cubic term. Thus
$$
\boxed{\frac{\ln(1+x^3)}{\cosh x}=x^3-\frac12x^5-\frac12x^6+O(x^7).}
$$
The numerator in the original PDF is $\ln(1+x^3)$; the cubic power is missing from the local TeX transcription.

