= Solution
{parent=i}

Expand <hyperbolic sine> in <exponential functions>:
$$
e^x\sinh3x=\frac12(e^{4x}-e^{-2x}).
$$
Integrating each term gives the <indefinite integral>
$$
\boxed{\int e^x\sinh3x\,dx=\frac18e^{4x}+\frac14e^{-2x}+C.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For <integration by parts>, take $u=\arctan x$ and $dv=x^{-2}\,dx$. Then $du=dx/(1+x^2)$ and $v=-1/x$, so
$$
\int\frac{\arctan x}{x^2}\,dx=-\frac{\arctan x}{x}+\int\frac{dx}{x(1+x^2)}.
$$
Use <partial fraction decomposition> $1/[x(1+x^2)]=1/x-x/(1+x^2)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int\frac{\arctan x}{x^2}\,dx=-\frac{\arctan x}{x}+\ln|x|-\frac12\ln(1+x^2)+C,\qquad x\ne0.}
$$
The <constant of integration> may be chosen independently on the positive and negative intervals.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use <integration by substitution> $u=\ln x$, $du=dx/x$. The bounds become $3$ and $4$, and the remaining denominator factorizes as $(u-1)(u-2)$. The <partial fraction decomposition> is
$$
\frac{3u-4}{(u-1)(u-2)}=\frac1{u-1}+\frac2{u-2}.
$$
Consequently the <definite integral> is
$$
\boxed{\left[\ln(u-1)+2\ln(u-2)\right]_3^4
=\ln\frac32+2\ln2=\ln6.}
$$
All logarithm arguments here are positive on the integration interval.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Two applications of <integration by parts> lower the exponent by two. First, the sine boundary term vanishes:
$$
I_n=[x^{2n}\sin x]_0^\pi-2n\int_0^\pi x^{2n-1}\sin x\,dx
=-2n\int_0^\pi x^{2n-1}\sin x\,dx.
$$
For $n\ge1$, integrate the last expression once more:
$$
\int_0^\pi x^{2n-1}\sin x\,dx=[-x^{2n-1}\cos x]_0^\pi
+(2n-1)\int_0^\pi x^{2n-2}\cos x\,dx
=\pi^{2n-1}+(2n-1)I_{n-1}.
$$
Thus the <recurrence relation> is
$$
\boxed{I_n=-2n\pi^{2n-1}-2n(2n-1)I_{n-1},\qquad n\ge1.}
$$
The starting value is $I_0=\int_0^\pi\cos x\,dx=0$. Successive applications give $I_1=-2\pi$ and $I_2=-4\pi^3+24\pi$, followed by
$$
\boxed{I_3=-6\pi^5+120\pi^3-720\pi.}
$$

