= Solution
{parent=i}

Since $A^2=-I$, the <matrix inverse> is $A^{-1}=-A$. The <orthogonal matrix> condition also gives $A^{-1}=A^{\mathsf T}$, so $A^{\mathsf T}=-A$. Thus $A$ is <skew-symmetric>, forcing $a=0$ and $c=-b$. Its columns must be <unit vectors>, giving $b^2=1$. Therefore the two possibilities are
$$
\boxed{A=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}\quad\text{or}\quad A=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Both satisfy the original conditions by direct multiplication. They are the two <orthogonal skew-symmetric matrices> in two dimensions, representing opposite quarter-turn <rotations>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For either $A$ from part (i), choose
$$
\boxed{B=-\frac{I+A}{\sqrt2}.}
$$
Indeed, using $A^2=-I$ and $A^{\mathsf T}=-A$,
$$
B^2=\frac{I+2A+A^2}{2}=A,\qquad
B^{\mathsf T}B=\frac{(I-A)(I+A)}2=I.
$$
Its <matrix trace> is $-2/\sqrt2=-\sqrt2<0$. Explicitly, for the two $A$ matrices in the order displayed above, the corresponding <orthogonal matrices> are
$$
\boxed{B=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\-1&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}-1&-1\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Their negatives have positive <matrix trace> and would not meet the additional sign condition.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Each prediction is a linear combination of the constant parameter and the coefficient of $x_i$. Thus the <design matrix> is
$$
\boxed{M=\begin{pmatrix}1&x_1\\1&x_2\\\vdots&\vdots\\1&x_n\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Its $i$th row multiplied by the parameter <vector> $(\alpha,\beta)^{\mathsf T}$ gives the required prediction $\alpha+\beta x_i$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Compute the two sides of the <normal equations for linear least squares> and divide by $n$:
$$
\begin{pmatrix}1&\overline x\\\overline x&\overline{x^2}\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\alpha\\\beta\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}\overline y\\\overline{xy}\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The first equation gives $\alpha=\overline y-\beta\overline x$. Substitute this into the second and solve:
$$
\boxed{\beta=\frac{\overline{xy}-\overline x\,\overline y}{\overline{x^2}-\overline x^{\,2}},\qquad
\alpha=\frac{\overline{x^2}\,\overline y-\overline x\,\overline{xy}}{\overline{x^2}-\overline x^{\,2}}.}
$$
These <simple linear regression> coefficients are unique provided the observed distances are not all equal, since
$$
\overline{x^2}-\overline x^{\,2}=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\overline x)^2>0.
$$
The condition $n\ge2$ alone does not guarantee this. If all $x_i=x_0$, the <design matrix> has <matrix rank> one and the valid alternative is the family
$$
\boxed{\alpha+x_0\beta=\overline y,\qquad\beta\text{ arbitrary}.}
$$
In this <rank-deficient ordinary least squares> case, the fitted predictions are uniquely $\overline y$ but the two parameters cannot be separately determined.

