{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For every integer $n$, $\cos((2n-1)\pi)=-1$. The <series> is therefore the negative of the <harmonic series>:
$$
\sum_{n=1}^N\frac{\cos((2n-1)\pi)}n=-\sum_{n=1}^N\frac1n.
$$
For a direct divergence argument, each block $2^{j-1}<n\le2^j$ contributes at least $2^{j-1}/2^j=1/2$ to the positive sum. Its <partial sums> grow without bound, so
$$
\boxed{\text{the given series diverges to }-\infty.}
$$
Despite the odd multiples of $\pi$, the signs do not alternate.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For each fixed $n$, the inner <geometric series> sums to $2^{-n}/(1-1/2)=2^{1-n}$. Summing this second <geometric series> gives
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^\infty\sum_{m=n}^\infty2^{-m}=\sum_{n=0}^\infty2^{1-n}=4.}
$$
All terms are nonnegative, so there is no conditional-rearrangement issue; the iterated sum is finite and the <double series> converges. Equivalently, collecting equal values of $m$ gives $\sum_{m=0}^\infty(m+1)2^{-m}=4$.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The positive <polynomial root> is $x_*=\varepsilon+\sqrt{1+\varepsilon^2}$. Its <Taylor expansion> near zero is
$$
x_*=1+\varepsilon+\frac12\varepsilon^2-\frac18\varepsilon^4+O(\varepsilon^6).
$$
The starting error is therefore $x_0-x_*=-\varepsilon-\tfrac12\varepsilon^2+O(\varepsilon^4)$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{|x_0-x_*|\sim|\varepsilon|,\qquad n_0=1.}
$$
For signed $\varepsilon$, the magnitude scaling should be written with $|\varepsilon|$; this is the precise interpretation of the proportionality in the question.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Newton root-finding iteration> for this <quadratic polynomial> is
$$
x_{k+1}=x_k-\frac{x_k^2-2\varepsilon x_k-1}{2(x_k-\varepsilon)}
=\frac{x_k^2+1}{2(x_k-\varepsilon)}.
$$
At $x_0=1$, it gives $x_1=1/(1-\varepsilon)$. Its <geometric series> expansion is $1+\varepsilon+\varepsilon^2+\varepsilon^3+O(\varepsilon^4)$. Subtracting the expansion of the <polynomial root> yields
$$
x_1-x_*=\frac12\varepsilon^2+\varepsilon^3+O(\varepsilon^4),
\qquad\boxed{|x_1-x_*|\sim\frac12|\varepsilon|^2,\qquad n_1=2.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the <exact Newton error identity for a quadratic> to expose the cancellation without a long quotient expansion. Put $e_k=x_k-x_*$. Since $f(x_*)=0$, direct expansion gives
$$
f(x_k)=2(x_*-\varepsilon)e_k+e_k^2,\qquad
e_{k+1}=e_k-\frac{2(x_*-\varepsilon)e_k+e_k^2}{2(x_*-\varepsilon+e_k)}
=\frac{e_k^2}{2(x_k-\varepsilon)}.
$$
From part (ii), $e_1=\tfrac12\varepsilon^2+O(\varepsilon^3)$ and $x_1-\varepsilon\to1$. It follows that
$$
e_2=\frac{\frac14\varepsilon^4+O(\varepsilon^5)}{2(1+O(\varepsilon^2))}
=\frac18\varepsilon^4+O(\varepsilon^5).
$$
Thus the stronger result is
$$
\boxed{|x_2-x_*|\sim\frac18|\varepsilon|^4,\qquad n_2=4>3.}
$$
The vanishing lower-order terms are a concrete instance of <quadratic convergence>: to leading order, this step squares the previous error.

