= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $d=b-a$ and $h=d/n$. The required <Riemann sum> is
$$
S_n=h\sum_{k=1}^n(a+kh)^2
=h\left[na^2+2ah\sum_{k=1}^nk+h^2\sum_{k=1}^nk^2\right].
$$
Using $\sum k=n(n+1)/2$ and the given sum of squares,
$$
\boxed{S_n=a^2d+ad^2\frac{n+1}{n}+d^3\frac{(n+1)(2n+1)}{6n^2}.}
$$
An equivalent expression separates its <limit> and finite-$n$ corrections:
$$
S_n=\frac{b^3-a^3}{3}+\frac{(b-a)(b^2-a^2)}{2n}+\frac{(b-a)^3}{6n^2}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}S_n=\frac{b^3-a^3}{3}=\int_a^bx^2\,dx.}
$$
The function is continuous, so the <Riemann sums> converge to its <definite integral>, the desired <area> under the <parabola>. Both corrections are positive when $0<a<b$, agreeing with the geometric fact that these right-endpoint rectangles overestimate an increasing curve.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Apply the <Leibniz integral rule>, including both moving boundaries and the explicit parameter in the integrand. The upper bound has <derivative> $-\sin x$, the lower bound has <derivative> $\cos x$, and the integrand has <partial derivative> $-t^4e^{-xt^4}$ with respect to $x$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f'(x)=-\sin x\,e^{-x\cos^4x}-\cos x\,e^{-x\sin^4x}-\int_{\sin x}^{\cos x}t^4e^{-xt^4}\,dt.}
$$
The formula remains valid when the bounds coincide or occur in reversed order, by the oriented <definite integral> convention. The integrand and its <partial derivative> are continuous on every finite integration interval.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the first <definite integral>, use <integration by substitution> $t=\tan x$, with $dx=dt/(1+t^2)$, $\cos^2x=1/(1+t^2)$ and $\sin^2x=t^2/(1+t^2)$. This gives
$$
I=\int_0^\infty\frac{dt}{p+qt^2}
=\frac1{\sqrt{pq}}\left[\arctan\left(t\sqrt{\frac qp}\right)\right]_0^\infty
=\boxed{\frac\pi{2\sqrt{pq}}.}
$$
For the second calculation, let $D=p\cos^2x+q\sin^2x$. Since $p,q>0$, this denominator is bounded away from zero; on compact positive parameter ranges the differentiated integrands have uniform bounds. Thus <differentiation under the integral sign> is justified and gives
$$
I_p=-\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{\cos^2x}{D^2}\,dx=-\frac\pi{4p^{3/2}q^{1/2}},\qquad
I_q=-\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{\sin^2x}{D^2}\,dx=-\frac\pi{4p^{1/2}q^{3/2}}.
$$
Adding the two equations and using the <Pythagorean trigonometric identity> in the numerators yields $J=-I_p-I_q$, hence
$$
\boxed{J=\frac{\pi(p+q)}{4(pq)^{3/2}}.}
$$
For $p=q$, this becomes $\pi/(2p^2)$, agreeing with direct integration of the constant integrand.
